---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PlatformGraph from '../components/PlatformGraph.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="al-Nizam — an operating layer between operator and AI"
  description="al-Nizam is the discipline AI tools demand you provide yourself — built as a framework so you don't have to."
>
  <!-- Full-viewport entry — the al-Mushahada graph IS the entry visual (iff.com-class);
       headline + Enter CTA overlaid on it, the site content sits below the fold. The
       graph's own stats/readout/arc chrome is suppressed here (splash mode) via CSS. -->
  <section class="entry">
    <!-- Pre-rendered cinematic loop of the al-Mushahada graph (recorded via
         PlatformGraph's ?record harness). WebGL is ATMOSPHERE here, not a live
         figure: a recorded loop gives full art-direction, zero runtime camera
         cost, and a mobile / reduced-motion-safe poster. The interactive graph
         lives in its own section below the fold, where drag-to-orbit is the point. -->
    <video class="entry-video" loop muted playsinline preload="none"
           poster="/heroes/hero-poster.jpg">
      <!-- No src in markup: JS injects it ONLY on desktop / motion-ok / not-save-data.
           Phones (<=760px), prefers-reduced-motion, and data-saver connections keep
           the poster still and NEVER fetch the ~19MB loop. -->
      <source data-src="/heroes/hero.webm" type="video/webm" />
    </video>
    <div class="entry-overlay">
      <h1 class="hero-headline">{"Working alone at the scale of an institution.".split(" ").map((word, i) => (<><span class="hero-word" style={`animation-delay:${(0.05 * i).toFixed(2)}s`}>{word}</span>{" "}</>))}</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">
        al-Nizam is the operating layer between you and AI that holds continuity,
        governance, and accountability structurally — so the operator does not
        have to enforce them by remembering to.
      </p>
      <a class="enter-cta" href="#site">Enter <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article id="site">
    <p>
      AI changed the math of knowledge work, but not in the direction the
      marketing suggested. It did not give us a team. It gave us a more
      powerful, more confident, more fluent version of the same problem we
      already had: more output than we could govern, more decisions than we
      could track, more state than any one operator could hold across sessions.
    </p>

    <p>
      The right response is not better prompts. It is not a better model. It is
      an <strong>operating layer</strong> between the operator and the AI — one
      that holds governance, continuity, and accountability structurally. That
      is what al-Nizam is.
    </p>

    <h2>What al-Nizam already operates that AI tools don't</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Memory and pipeline.</strong> State lives in files, not in any
        vendor's model. If every AI provider went dark tomorrow, the work would
        still be there. Sessions resume coherently — across days, across
        machines.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Continuity across multiple machines.</strong> Two strands of
        work on two machines compose into one coherent operator-perspective,
        because the framework's metabolism propagates state deliberately and
        gradually — without forcing alignment that would destroy what diverged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Roles, not personas.</strong> One operator engages many
        disciplines — engineering, governance, security, content, research —
        without confusing them. The roles are structural; the right discipline
        fires for the work at hand.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Calibrated uncertainty.</strong> A protocol layer, not a
        personality trait. The framework forces the system to say what it does
        not know — and the operator to label confidence on any claim that
        isn't directly verified from source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bounded scope per decision.</strong> Substantive moves pause
        before execution to verify they are <em>best, simplest, and most
        scalable</em> — not just available.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Honest reckoning after the work.</strong> Declared versus
        delivered — not self-aggrandizing summary but honest accounting,
        recorded permanently in append-only logs.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- The interactive al-Mushahada graph — its own surface, where drag-to-orbit
         and click-a-node are the point (the hero above is the pre-rendered loop). -->
    <PlatformGraph />

    <h2>Read</h2>

    <p>
      The <a href="/leadership/">Leadership Channel</a> publishes long-form
      pieces examining the disciplines al-Nizam has evolved. Each piece takes
      a problem the world is already struggling with — AI amnesia, governance,
      cost, trust — and shows what an operating layer makes possible.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/leadership/memory-and-pipeline-in-practice/">
      <strong>Start here: "AI forgets everything the moment I close the tab" →</strong></a>
    </p>

    <hr />

    <p class="lead">
      <em>al-Nizam — Arabic for "the order, the system, the discipline" — is
      a personal operating framework. It runs in the layer between an operator
      and the AI tools they use. It enforces continuity, governance, and
      accountability structurally. It is designed to scale: one specialist,
      one generalist, one team, one enterprise — the same primitives,
      tier-positioned to the operator class.</em>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Full-bleed hero band — breaks out of <main>'s 56rem column to viewport width.
     Background is the same night as the header/body, so it reads as one seamless
     brand field flowing from the header into the immersive graph below. */
  /* Full-viewport entry: the graph fills the viewport as the entry visual; the
     headline + Enter CTA overlay it; the site content sits below the fold. */
  .entry {
    position: relative;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: calc(50% - 50vw);
    margin-right: calc(50% - 50vw);
    height: 100vh;
    min-height: 620px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* Pre-rendered hero loop as full-bleed background; object-fit: cover fills any
     viewport aspect (the recorded buffer is 16:9). Sits behind the text overlay. */
  .entry-video { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; z-index: 0; }

  /* overlay — centered headline + Enter, scrim for legibility, click-through to the
     graph everywhere except the text/CTA (pointer-events). */
  .entry-overlay {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 2;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; align-items: center;
    text-align: center; padding: 1.5rem;
    /* Lighter, tighter scrim — just enough to seat the headline, letting the larger
       glowing graph read brightly around it (the graph is the awe; the scrim only
       protects the text). Legibility comes from the text-shadow below, not a heavy veil. */
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse 58% 46% at 50% 47%, rgba(14,18,32,0.62) 0%, rgba(14,18,32,0.22) 58%, rgba(14,18,32,0) 100%);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .entry-overlay .hero-headline,
  .entry-overlay .hero-lead {
    text-shadow: 0 1px 22px rgba(14,18,32,0.85), 0 1px 4px rgba(14,18,32,0.9);
  }
  .entry-overlay > * { pointer-events: auto; max-width: 44rem; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; }

  .entry-overlay .hero-headline {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 3.4rem);
    line-height: 1.12;
    /* `auto` left/right keeps the headline block centered — a bare `0 0` here
       would override the margin:auto centering inherited from `.hero > *`,
       which is what pinned the title left-of-center. */
    margin: 0 auto 1.1rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* Kinetic composition — the headline assembles word by word on load, a quiet
     nod to al-Nizam's own thesis: parts compose into a whole. Then the lead and
     CTA settle in beneath it. Purely additive: with prefers-reduced-motion the
     whole hero is present and static from the first frame. */
  .hero-word {
    display: inline-block;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(0.5em);
    animation: heroWordIn 0.62s cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.7, 0.2, 1) forwards;
  }

  @keyframes heroWordIn {
    to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  .hero-lead {
    opacity: 0;
    animation: heroSettleIn 0.7s ease 0.62s forwards;
  }

  /* CTA fades opacity only — NOT transform — so the entrance's `forwards` fill
     doesn't pin transform and clobber the hover lift defined below. */
  .enter-cta {
    opacity: 0;
    animation: heroFadeIn 0.7s ease 0.82s forwards;
  }

  @keyframes heroSettleIn {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(0.4em); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  @keyframes heroFadeIn {
    to { opacity: 1; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hero-word,
    .hero-lead,
    .enter-cta {
      animation: none;
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  .hero-lead {
    font-size: 1.18rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 auto 1.6rem;
  }

  /* Single CTA — gold pill with an organic, asymmetric corner (the IFF signature
     touch) + letter-spaced uppercase label and an arrow. */
  .enter-cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 2rem;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: #0E1220;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: 2rem 2rem 2rem 0.4rem;
    transition: background 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;
  }

  .enter-cta:hover {
    background: var(--color-accent-hover);
    color: #0E1220;
    transform: translateY(-1px);
  }

  /* Homepage thesis prose shares the al-Mushahada content column (48rem) so the
     text below the graph aligns with the stats/readout/arc above it, instead of
     sitting in the narrower 38rem reading column the global stylesheet sets. */
  article { max-width: 48rem; }
  /* Anchor lands the FIRST line clear of the sticky (condensed) header — the JS
     handler below refines this after the header settles; this is the no-JS fallback. */
  #site { scroll-margin-top: 72px; }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .entry { min-height: 520px; }
    .hero-lead { font-size: 1.08rem; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Hero video loads + plays ONLY where it's worth it: desktop, motion allowed, and
  // not a data-saver connection. On phones (<=760px), prefers-reduced-motion, or
  // Save-Data we leave the poster still in place and NEVER fetch the ~19MB loop (the
  // <source> carries no src until we set it). Cheapest thing that still looks
  // intentional — the mobile / data win. Desktop gets the full cinematic loop.
  const heroVideo = document.querySelector('.entry-video');
  if (heroVideo) {
    const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    const smallScreen = window.matchMedia('(max-width: 760px)').matches;
    const conn = navigator.connection || {};
    const heavyOk = !reduce && !smallScreen && !conn.saveData;
    if (heavyOk) {
      const source = heroVideo.querySelector('source[data-src]');
      if (source && !source.src) {
        source.src = source.dataset.src;
        heroVideo.load();
        heroVideo.play().catch(() => {});
      }
    }
    // else: poster stays; zero video bytes fetched.
  }

  // Enter CTA — scroll to the article's FIRST line, clear of the sticky header.
  // A plain #site hash-jump lands past the first lines because the header is sticky
  // AND condenses on scroll (reflow mid-scroll). We scroll with a live header-height
  // offset, then re-correct once the header has settled into its condensed height.
  const cta = document.querySelector('.enter-cta');
  const site = document.getElementById('site');
  const header = document.querySelector('header.site-header');
  if (cta && site) {
    const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    const go = () => {
      const h = (header ? header.getBoundingClientRect().height : 0) + 14;
      const y = window.scrollY + site.getBoundingClientRect().top - h;
      window.scrollTo({ top: Math.max(0, y), behavior: reduce ? 'auto' : 'smooth' });
    };
    cta.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      go();
      // Re-correct after the sticky header finishes condensing (~0.25s transition).
      window.setTimeout(go, 600);
    });
  }
</script>
